            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"A temporal expression denoting suddenness or brevity, commonly applied contextually to the suddenness of divine judgment, the brevity of earthly suffering compared to eternity, or the instantaneous nature of the resurrection (1 Cor 15).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A short time expression and prophetic pointer to a singular eschatological event—the sudden destruction of Babylon the Great aligned with the resurrection—which is typified by unusually brief intervals in prophecy.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A literal expression of brevity or suddenness, applied contextually to various events including judgment and resurrection.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A typological reference to the specific eschatological timing of Babylon's destruction and the resurrection.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes generic pastoral or existential uses of 'moment' (e.g., brevity of life or affliction), while expanding the prophetic scope to include other unusually brief intervals (such as 'one hour' or 'one day' in Revelation 18) as pointers to the same event.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book restricts a generic temporal word to a highly specific, unified prophetic type, though it does not deny the base literal sense of brevity.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book preserves the consensus recognition of 'moment' as a short time expression associated with sudden judgment and resurrection, but refines the concept by merging these themes into a singular typological event and extending it to encompass other unusually brief prophetic intervals.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Standard hermeneutics treats 'moment' as a generic temporal descriptor dependent entirely on its immediate context; fusing distinct uses in Isaiah, Job, 1 Corinthians, and Revelation into a single, fixed typological event over-systematizes the term.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
